The '''Principles of the Treaty of Waitangi Bill''', commonly known as the '''Treaty Principles Bill''', was a [[government bill (law)|government Bill]]<ref name="Parliament" /> that aimed to define the [[principles of the Treaty of Waitangi]] and put them to a nationwide referendum for confirmation.<ref name="RNZ Feb 2024 launch">{{Cite news |title=ACT launches Treaty Principles Bill information campaign |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/508579/act-launches-treaty-principles-bill-information-campaign |access-date=7 February 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=7 February 2024 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240207015928/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/508579/act-launches-treaty-principles-bill-information-campaign |archive-date=7 February 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> The [[ACT New Zealand|ACT New Zealand Party]], who introduced this Bill, campaigned against the [[co-governance]] policies of the [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Sixth Labour Government]] and advocated a binding referendum on co-governance, claiming that the current principles distorted the original intent of the Treaty of Waitangi and created an inequality in peoples' rights.<ref name=Sherman2022>{{Cite news |last=Sherman |first=Maiki |title=ACT Party wants referendum on co-governance with Māori |url= https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/03/24/act-party-wants-referendum-on-co-governance-with-maori/ |access-date=27 January 2023 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date= 24 March 2022 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230127031536/https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/03/24/act-party-wants-referendum-on-co-governance-with-maori/ |archive-date=27 January 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|National-led coalition government]] partners [[New Zealand National Party|National]] and [[New Zealand First]] did not support the bill past its first reading and referral to a [[New Zealand House of Representatives committees#Select committees|select committee]],<ref name="resolute">{{Cite news |last=Hu |first=Justin |date=28 April 2024 |title=National 'resolute' on Treaty Principles Bill stance – minister |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/28/national-resolute-on-treaty-principles-bill-stance-minister/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240428050256/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/04/28/national-resolute-on-treaty-principles-bill-stance-minister/ |archive-date=28 April 2024 |access-date=7 June 2024 |work=[[1News]]}}</ref> and some legal critics argued the bill sought to undermine Māori rights and disrupt established interpretations of the Treaty.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-13 |title=Top lawyers tell PM to 'abandon' Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360486124/top-lawyers-tell-pm-abandon-treaty-principles-bill |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250125123114/https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360486124/top-lawyers-tell-pm-abandon-treaty-principles-bill |archive-date=25 January 2025 |access-date=2024-11-25 |website=www.stuff.co.nz}}</ref> On its second reading, which occurred on 10 April 2025, the bill was voted down.<ref name="RNZ 10 Apr 2025">{{Cite news |title='Watch this space': Seymour on if voted down Treaty Principles Bill will return |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/557766/watch-live-treaty-principles-bill-debate-at-second-reading-in-parliament |access-date=10 April 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=10 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250410232416/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/557766/watch-this-space-seymour-on-if-voted-down-treaty-principles-bill-will-return |archive-date=10 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="BBC News 10 Apr 2025">{{Cite news |last1=Armstrong |first1=Kathryn |title=New Zealand rejects rights bill after widespread outrage |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8je9013m0ro |access-date=10 April 2025 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=10 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250410204935/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8je9013m0ro |archive-date=10 April 2025}}</ref>

== Treaty of Waitangi ==
{{main|Treaty of Waitangi}}
The Treaty of Waitangi, including a Preamble and Three Articles in English and Māori, was signed in 1840 by Māori chiefs and representatives of the British Crown. Notably, 39 chiefs signed the English version of the Treaty, while over 500 signed the Māori version, which is referred to as {{lang|mi|Te Tiriti o Waitangi}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Te Tiriti O Waitangi: Why We Have It, What It Says And Why It Matters Now |url=https://groundwork.org.nz/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Groundwork-Te-Tiriti-Explainer.pdf |access-date=2024-11-25 |website=Groundwork.org.nz |type=PDF |archive-date=12 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240812123920/https://groundwork.org.nz/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Groundwork-Te-Tiriti-Explainer.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> Some words in the English treaty did not translate directly into the written [[Māori language]] of the time, and thus, the Māori text is not an exact translation of the English text.

The most significant differences in the two versions of the Treaty particularly concern sovereignty, government, and governance in Articles 1 and 2.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Orange |first=Claudia |title=The Treaty of Waitangi |date=1987 |publisher=Allen & Unwin : Port Nicolson Press, with assistance from the Historical Publications Branch, Dept. of Internal Affairs, Wellington |isbn=978-0-86861-427-4 |location=Wellington; Winchester, Mass., USA}}</ref><ref name="treaty1">{{Cite encyclopedia|first=Claudia|last=Orange|title=Treaty of Waitangi – Interpretations of the Treaty of Waitangi|url=http://www.teara.govt.nz/en/treaty-of-waitangi/page-2|encyclopedia=Te Ara: The Encyclopedia of New Zealand|access-date=1 January 2025|date=20 June 2012|archive-date=27 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230227173903/https://teara.govt.nz/en/treaty-of-waitangi/page-2|url-status=live}}</ref> "Sovereignty" was translated as "[[kāwanatanga]]" which means 'governance' or 'government,' and many chiefs believed they were ceding the government of the country but maintaining the rights to manage their affairs. "Undisturbed possession of properties" was translated as "[[tino rangatiratanga]]" of "[[taonga]] katoa", This means 'chieftainship/full authority' over 'all treasured things'.<ref name="Meaning-Tribunal">{{Cite web |url= https://www.waitangitribunal.govt.nz/treaty-of-waitangi/meaning-of-the-treaty/ |title= Meaning of the Treaty |year= 2011 |publisher=Waitangi Tribunal |access-date= 12 July 2011 |url-status= live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160708084042/http://www.waitangitribunal.govt.nz/treaty-of-waitangi/meaning-of-the-treaty/ |archive-date= 8 July 2016}}</ref><ref name="B&T II">{{Cite book |last=Newman |first=Keith |title=Bible & Treaty, Missionaries among the Māori – a new perspective |year = 2010 |orig-year = 2010 |publisher=Penguin |isbn=978-0143204084}} pp 20-116</ref><ref name="Explanation">{{Cite news |title=Explained: The Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360487289/explained-treaty-principles-bill |first=Lloyd |last=Burr |access-date=16 November 2024 |website=www.stuff.co.nz |date=14 November 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|first=John|last=Moorfield|title=Kāwanatanga |url=https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kawanatanga |publisher=Te Aka Māori Dictionary |access-date=1 January 2025}}</ref>

Aotearoa New Zealand is a signatory to the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples ([[Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples|UNDRIP]]), which emphasises the importance of using treaties and agreements in Indigenous languages. Despite not being incorporated into law, UNDRIP has begun to influence policy and judicial decisions in New Zealand. For example, the Declaration has been referenced in several decisions of the Supreme Court of New Zealand and extensively in findings by the Waitangi Tribunal.<ref>{{Cite web |title=UNDRIP in other jurisdictions |url=https://antar.org.au/issues/undrip/undrip-in-other-jurisdictions/ |access-date=2024-11-25 |website=ANTAR |language=en-AU |archive-date=3 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241103025618/https://antar.org.au/issues/undrip/undrip-in-other-jurisdictions/ |url-status=live }}</ref> However, in November 2023, the [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|National-led coalition government]] agreed not to recognise the Declaration as having any binding legal effect on New Zealand as part of a coalition agreement. This coalition government also agreed to stop all work on [[He Puapua]], which is a government commissioned report on implementing the goals of [[UNDRIP]] in New Zealand.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Coalition Agreement with National & New Zealand First |url=https://assets.nationbuilder.com/nationalparty/pages/18466/attachments/original/1700778597/NZFirst_Agreement_2.pdf?1700778597 |publisher=Government of New Zealand |access-date=4 January 2025 |archive-date=9 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240109140346/https://assets.nationbuilder.com/nationalparty/pages/18466/attachments/original/1700778597/NZFirst_Agreement_2.pdf?1700778597 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Principles===
In 1975, the New Zealand Parliament passed the [[Treaty of Waitangi Act 1975]], which established the [[Waitangi Tribunal]] and introduced the phrase "the [[principles of the Treaty of Waitangi]]." The principles were not defined, as the tribunal was intended to interpret them and apply them based on the intentions of the treaty,<ref name="Explanation" /> though they have also been interpreted by the [[Judiciary of New Zealand|Courts]] and the [[Monarchy of New Zealand|Crown]].<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |title= Principles of the Treaty of Waitangi – ngā mātāpono o te Tiriti o Waitangi |url=https://teara.govt.nz/en/principles-of-the-treaty-of-waitangi-nga-matapono-o-te-tiriti-o-waitangi |encyclopedia=[[Te Ara: The Encyclopedia of New Zealand|Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand]] |access-date=16 November 2024 |archive-date=20 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240420001354/https://teara.govt.nz/en/principles-of-the-treaty-of-waitangi-nga-matapono-o-te-tiriti-o-waitangi |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |title= What are the treaty principles? |url=https://teara.govt.nz/en/principles-of-the-treaty-of-waitangi-nga-matapono-o-te-tiriti-o-waitangi/page-1 |encyclopedia=[[Te Ara: The Encyclopedia of New Zealand|Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand]] |access-date=2024-11-16 |archive-date=2024-06-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240613020107/https://teara.govt.nz/en/principles-of-the-treaty-of-waitangi-nga-matapono-o-te-tiriti-o-waitangi/page-1 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |title= Treaty principles developed by the Crown |url=https://teara.govt.nz/en/principles-of-the-treaty-of-waitangi-nga-matapono-o-te-tiriti-o-waitangi/page-5 |encyclopedia=[[Te Ara: The Encyclopedia of New Zealand|Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand]] |access-date=16 November 2024}}</ref>

== Background ==
In March 2022, leader of the [[Libertarianism|libertarian]] [[ACT New Zealand|ACT Party]] [[David Seymour]] announced a policy of redefining the [[principles of the Treaty of Waitangi]] in law during the next parliamentary term. The law would only come into effect following confirmation by a referendum on Māori [[co-governance]] that would be held at the 2026 general election. He said that agreeing to hold the referendum would be a condition for forming a government with the centre-right [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]]. Seymour argued that the treaty was not a partnership between the [[New Zealand Crown]] and [[Māori people|Māori]], and therefore co-governance arrangements were not a "necessary extension" of it. He also claimed that co-governance created resentment.<ref name=Sherman2022 /> ACT's proposed law would affect co-governance arrangements at several [[Crown Research Institute]]s, [[state-owned enterprise]]s and healthcare providers such as [[Te Aka Whai Ora]] (the Māori Health Authority). However, Seymour indicated that it would preserve existing co-governance arrangements with the [[Waikato (iwi)|Waikato]], [[Ngāi Tahu]], [[Tūhoe]] and [[Whanganui]] iwi.<ref name="Newshub Te Tiriti">{{Cite news |last= Forbes |first= Mihirangi |title= Act Party – rewrites Te Tiriti interpretation |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/03/act-s-david-seymour-explains-why-he-wants-m-ori-co-governance-referendum.html |access-date= 27 January 2023 |work= [[Newshub]] |date=29 March 2022 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230127031533/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/03/act-s-david-seymour-explains-why-he-wants-m-ori-co-governance-referendum.html |archive-date=27 January 2023|url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[Māori Party]] co-leader [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]] said ACT's proposals emboldened racism. Professor [[Linda Tuhiwai Smith]] said they reflected a "[[Pākehā]]" unwillingness to share power.<ref name=Sherman2022 /> Waikato leader Rahui Papa claimed that they clashed with the second and third articles of the treaty, which (he argued) guaranteed Māori participation in the social sector.<ref name="Newshub Te Tiriti" /> In response, Prime Minister [[Jacinda Ardern]] reiterated the [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Labour Government]]'s commitment to co-governance arrangements. National Party leader [[Christopher Luxon]] refused to commit to a referendum on co-governance, but acknowledged that further clarity on co-governance was needed.<ref name=Sherman2022 />

In October 2022, ACT released a discussion document entitled "Democracy or co-government?", which proposed a Treaty Principles Bill that would end the focus on partnership between Māori and the Crown and interpret "[[Tino Rangatiratanga]]" solely as property rights, while accepted meanings range from "absolute soveriengty" to "self-government". The proposed Treaty Principles Bill did not mention Māori, the Crown, iwi, and [[hapū]] (subgroups), but referred only to "New Zealanders". Seymour refused to identify whom his party had consulted when developing its co-governance and Treaty of Waitangi policies, particularly its redefinition of "[[Tino Rangatiratanga]]" as property rights. As part of ACT's "colour-blind" policies, its social-development spokesperson [[Karen Chhour]] advocated the abolition of Te Aka Whai Ora.<ref name="Stuff 10 Oct 2022">{{Cite news |last1= McConnell |first1=Glenn |title=ACT increases 'co-governance' focus, says Treaty referendum a 'bottom line' |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/130122915/act-increases-cogovernance-focus-says-treaty-referendum-a-bottom-line |access-date=28 January 2023 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=10 October 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230114091548/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/130122915/act-increases-cogovernance-focus-says-treaty-referendum-a-bottom-line |archive-date=14 January 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>

ACT party member of parliament [[Rodney Hide]] had introduced a similar [[Private member's bill#New Zealand|private bill]] in 2006, which was selected, debated, and rejected at that time.<ref name="nz-parliament-hide-bill-digest-2006">
