== Coalition agreement and 2024 leak ==
Following the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]], a [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|National-led coalition government]] was formed with the support of the ACT and [[New Zealand First]] parties.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Watch: Christopher Luxon, new ministers sworn in at Government House |url-status=live |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503352/watch-christopher-luxon-new-ministers-sworn-in-at-government-house |access-date=27 November 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=27 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231127054606/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503352/watch-christopher-luxon-new-ministers-sworn-in-at-government-house |archive-date=27 November 2023}}</ref> As part of ACT's coalition agreement with National, the parties agreed to introduce a Treaty Principles Bill based on existing ACT policy. National and New Zealand First agreed to support the bill up to the Parliamentary select committee stage.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Coalition Agreement with National & ACT |url= https://assets.nationbuilder.com/nationalparty/pages/18466/attachments/original/1700778592/National_ACT_Agreement.pdf?1700778592 |publisher=Government of New Zealand |access-date=30 December 2024 |archive-date=27 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240427152947/https://assets.nationbuilder.com/nationalparty/pages/18466/attachments/original/1700778592/National_ACT_Agreement.pdf?1700778592 |url-status=live }}</ref> In return, ACT dropped its demand for a referendum on the Treaty of Waitangi.<ref>{{Cite news |title=New government plans to review Treaty of Waitangi principles |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/503168/new-government-plans-to-review-treaty-of-waitangi-principles |access-date=10 June 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 November 2023 |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20231124032344/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/503168/new-government-plans-to-review-treaty-of-waitangi-principles |archive-date=24 November 2023}}</ref><ref name="Newsroom 24 Nov 2023">{{Cite news |last=Daalder |first=Mark |title=Which policies survived the negotiations – and which didn't |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2023/11/24/which-policies-survived-the-negotiations-and-which-didnt/ |access-date=10 June 2024|work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=24 November 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231124093235/https://newsroom.co.nz/2023/11/24/which-policies-survived-the-negotiations-and-which-didnt/|archive-date=24 November 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> {{As of|2025|01|lc=|post=,}} the bill under public consultation had to be submitted to a binding public referendum.<ref name="parliamentary-counsel-office-2024">
{{Cite book
 | author = Parliamentary Counsel Office
 | title = Principles of the Treaty of Waitangi Bill — Government Bill 94–1
 | date = 7 November 2024
 | publisher = Parliamentary Counsel Office
 | location = Wellington, New Zealand
 | url = http://www.nzlii.org/nz/legis/bill/pottowb2024369.pdf
 | access-date = 2025-01-21
}}
</ref>

On 19 January 2024, a [[Ministry of Justice (New Zealand)|Ministry of Justice]] memo on the proposed bill was leaked. The memo claimed that the proposed bill clashed with the text of the Treaty.<ref name="RNZ Feb 2024 launch" /> The proposed bill had three principles: that the New Zealand Government has the right to govern all New Zealanders; the New Zealand Government will honour all New Zealanders in the chieftainship of their land and all their property; and that all New Zealanders are equal under the law with the same rights and duties. The Ministry's paper expressed concerns that the proposed law would conflict with the rights and interests of Māori under the Treaty of Waitangi, that the Crown was trying to define Treaty principles without consulting with Māori, that the bill breached international agreements such as the [[International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights]], and that it infringed on the Māori right to self-determination. Seymour claimed the memo was a "natural reaction" from a bureaucracy that had "presided over increasing division over these issues", but stated the Ministry of Justice was not biased in its advice.<ref name="NZH 19 Jan 2024" />

The leak came on the eve of King [[Tūheitia]]'s national [[Hui (Māori assembly)|hui]] on 20 January. [[Minister of Justice (New Zealand)|Minister of Justice]] [[Paul Goldsmith (politician)|Paul Goldsmith]] confirmed that the Justice Ministry would investigate the leak and described the document as a draft that had not yet been considered by the Cabinet. Seymour accused the Ministry of being part of a bureaucracy that was "resistant to change." [[Rawiri Waititi]] and [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]], co-leaders of [[Te Pāti Māori]] (formerly the Māori Party), used the leak to rally opposition against the proposed constitutional changes.<ref name="NZH 19 Jan 2024" /><ref>{{Cite news |title=Government confirms leaked document was a ministry Treaty Principles bill memo |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/507090/government-confirms-leaked-document-was-a-ministry-treaty-principles-bill-memo |access-date=10 June 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=19 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240416222613/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/507090/government-confirms-leaked-document-was-a-ministry-treaty-principles-bill-memo |archive-date=16 April 2024}}</ref> Following the leak, staff from other government agencies who needed to look at Cabinet papers relating to the bill had to physically visit Ministry of Justice offices to look at hard copies to prevent leaks.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Daalder |first=Marc |title=Justice ministry battles leaks and ministers over Treaty bill |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/09/16/justice-ministry-battles-leaks-and-ministers-over-treaty-bill/ |access-date=18 September 2024 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=15 September 2024 |archive-date=18 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240918005355/https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/09/16/justice-ministry-battles-leaks-and-ministers-over-treaty-bill/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Release of draft ==
Following the leak, ACT released a draft of the proposed bill in February 2024. On 7 February 2024, they embarked on a public information campaign to promote it. The campaign included the creation of a new website called "treaty.nz," which has a Questions and Answers section outlining the party's approach to the principles of the Treaty of Waitangi and a video featuring Seymour. Seymour also contested claims that the government was trying to rewrite or abolish the Treaty of Waitangi.<ref name="RNZ Feb 2024 launch" /><ref name="treaty.nz">{{Cite web|url=https://www.treaty.nz/|access-date=5 April 2024 |title=Treaty Principles Bill|archive-date=5 April 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240405120238/https://www.treaty.nz/|url-status=live}}</ref>

ACT's proposed Treaty Principles Bill consists of three articles:<ref name="RNZ Feb 2024 launch" /><ref name="treaty.nz" />

'''Article 1'''
{{blockquote|{{langx|mi|Kawanatanga katoa o o ratou whenua.}}}}
{{blockquote|The New Zealand Government has the right to govern all New Zealanders.}}

'''Article 2'''
{{blockquote|{{langx|mi|Ki nga tangata katoa o Nu Tirani te tino rangatiratanga o o ratou whenua o ratou kainga me o ratou taonga katoa.}}}}
{{blockquote|The New Zealand Government will honour all New Zealanders in the chieftainship of their land and all their property.}}

'''Article 3'''
{{blockquote|{{langx|mi|A ratou nga tikanga katoa rite tahi.}}}}
{{blockquote|All New Zealanders are equal under the law with the same rights and duties.}}

The Treaty Principles Bill generated much controversy and drew criticism from Māori leaders and bodies, including the [[Waitangi Tribunal]], the opposition parties [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]], [[Green Party of Aotearoa|Green]], [[Te Pāti Māori]], religious leaders and lawyers.<ref name="RNZ 16 Aug 2024">{{Cite news |last1=Paewai |first1=Pokere |title=Waitangi Tribunal calls for Treaty Principles Bill to be abandoned in scathing report |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/525344/waitangi-tribunal-calls-for-treaty-principles-bill-to-be-abandoned-in-scathing-report |access-date=19 August 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240816082508/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/525344/waitangi-tribunal-calls-for-treaty-principles-bill-to-be-abandoned-in-scathing-report |archive-date=16 August 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Joint opposition statement">{{Cite web |title=Release: Labour, Greens and Te Pāti Māori call on the Prime Minister to block the Treaty Principles Bill |work=NZ Labour Party |url=https://www.labour.org.nz/news-release_labour_greens_and_te_p_ti_m_ori_call_on_the_prime_minister_to_block_the_treaty_principles_bill |publisher=[[New Zealand Labour Party]] |access-date=10 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241110235317/https://www.labour.org.nz/news-release_labour_greens_and_te_p_ti_m_ori_call_on_the_prime_minister_to_block_the_treaty_principles_bill |archive-date=10 November 2024 |date=7 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="NZH church leaders">{{Cite news |last1=Los'e |first1=Joseph |title=Treaty Principles Bill: 400 church leaders want David Seymour's bill voted down |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/kahu/treaty-principles-bill-hundreds-of-church-leaders-want-david-seymours-divisive-bill-voted-down/BG7C54DNK5GOZNMH6GGTIIEKMU/ |access-date=10 September 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=9 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240909030922/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/kahu/treaty-principles-bill-hundreds-of-church-leaders-want-david-seymours-divisive-bill-voted-down/BG7C54DNK5GOZNMH6GGTIIEKMU/ |archive-date=9 September 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="RNZ KCs">{{Cite news |title=Senior lawyers call for Treaty Principles Bill to be abandoned |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/533674/senior-lawyers-call-for-treaty-principles-bill-to-be-abandoned |access-date=13 November 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 November 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241113120813/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/533674/senior-lawyers-call-for-treaty-principles-bill-to-be-abandoned|archive-date=13 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> The Waitangi Tribunal found that "the Crown had breached the Treaty principles of partnership and reciprocity, active protection, good government, equity, redress, and the article 2 guarantee of rangatiratanga."<ref>{{Cite web |title=Tribunal releases report on Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.waitangitribunal.govt.nz/en/news-2/all-articles/news/tribunal-releases-report-on-treaty-principles-bill |access-date=2024-11-25 |website=Waitangi Tribunal |language=en-nz |archive-date=10 October 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241010131019/https://waitangitribunal.govt.nz/en/news-2/all-articles/news/tribunal-releases-report-on-treaty-principles-bill |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Legislative history ==
===Introduction===
On 9 September 2024, a draft outline of the Treaty Principles Bill was tabled at a Cabinet meeting, with its basic outline being signed off. Seymour confirmed that it would mention hapū (sub-group) and [[iwi]] (tribal) rights to [[tino rangatiratanga]] (self determination) and property ownership in Article 2. Cabinet agreed for the following principles to be included in the bill:<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Moir |first1=Jo |title=Treaty Principles Bill: Officials advise government against redefining principles |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/527682/treaty-principles-bill-officials-advise-government-against-redefining-principles |access-date=11 September 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=11 September 2024 |language=en-nz}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Next steps agreed for Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/next-steps-agreed-treaty-principles-bill |publisher=Beehive |access-date=11 September 2024 |archive-date=11 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240911005158/https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/next-steps-agreed-treaty-principles-bill |url-status=live }}</ref>

{{blockquote|1. Civil Government: The Government of New Zealand has full power to govern, and Parliament has full power to make laws. They do so in the best interests of everyone, and in accordance with the rule of law and the maintenance of a free and democratic society.
2. Rights of Hapū and Iwi Māori: The Crown recognises the rights that hapū and iwi had when they signed the Treaty. The Crown will respect and protect those rights. Those rights differ from the rights everyone has a reasonable expectation to enjoy only when they are specified in legislation, [[Treaty of Waitangi claims and settlements|Treaty settlements]], or other agreement with the Crown.
3. Right to Equality: Everyone is equal before the law and is entitled to the equal protection and equal benefit of the law without discrimination. Everyone is entitled to the equal enjoyment of the same fundamental human rights without discrimination.}}

While Seymour expressed hope that the coalition parties would support the bill after its first reading, [[Prime Minister of New Zealand|Prime Minister]] [[Christopher Luxon]] reiterated that the National Party would not support it beyond its first reading. [[New Zealand First]] also pledged not to support it beyond its first reading.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Treaty Principles Bill: Specific mention of iwi, hapū – Seymour |url-status=live |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/09/10/treaty-principles-bill-specific-mention-of-iwi-hapu-seymour/ |access-date=10 September 2024 |work=[[1News]] |date=10 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240910001802/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/09/10/treaty-principles-bill-specific-mention-of-iwi-hapu-seymour/ |archive-date=10 September 2024}}</ref><ref name="RNZ 11 Sep 2024">{{Cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Treaty Principles Bill to go through six-month select committee process |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/527629/treaty-principles-bill-to-go-through-six-month-select-committee-process |access-date=10 September 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=11 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240910235831/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/527629/treaty-principles-bill-to-go-through-six-month-select-committee-process |archive-date=10 September 2024}}</ref> The final version of the bill was to be considered by Cabinet again before its introduction to Parliament in November 2024.<ref name="RNZ 9 Sep 2024" /> Cabinet also agreed that the bill would undergo a six-month-long select committee process, concluding in May 2025.<ref name="RNZ 11 Sep 2024" /> On 5 November 2024, it was announced the bill's timetable was to introduce it to Parliament on 7 November, with the first reading debate advanced to the week of 11–15 November.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-07 |title=The Treaty Principles Bill has been released: Here's what's in it |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/533115/the-treaty-principles-bill-has-been-released-here-s-what-s-in-it |access-date=2024-11-07 |website=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz |archive-date=2024-11-09 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241109063921/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/533115/the-treaty-principles-bill-has-been-released-here-s-what-s-in-it |url-status=live }}</ref>

===First reading===
[[File:Principles of the Treaty of Waitangi Bill (Bill 94-1).pdf|thumb|Principles of the Treaty of Waitangi Bill]]
On 14 November, the Treaty Principles Bill passed its first reading despite opposition from the Labour, Māori and Green parties.<ref name="NZH 1st reading">{{Cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Treaty Principles Bill live updates: Te Pāti Māori MP Hana-Rawhiti Maipi-Clarke suspended as haka derails bill's first reading vote |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/treaty-principles-bill-government-to-face-grilling-ahead-of-vote/LHPY7ZGX3FDUNKUCIS6XJHC32I/ |access-date=15 November 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=14 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241115031326/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/treaty-principles-bill-government-to-face-grilling-ahead-of-vote/LHPY7ZGX3FDUNKUCIS6XJHC32I/ |url-status=live |archive-date=15 November 2024}}</ref> Support from the National Party and NZ First was guaranteed under the Coalition agreement, but only up to the second reading.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Explainer: The path ahead for the Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/11/15/explainer-the-path-ahead-for-the-treaty-principles-bill/ |access-date=25 November 2024 |work=[[1News]] |language=en |archive-date=27 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241127023152/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/11/15/explainer-the-path-ahead-for-the-treaty-principles-bill/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

During the debate, Labour MP [[Willie Jackson (politician)|Willie Jackson]] was ordered to leave by [[Speaker of the New Zealand House of Representatives|Speaker]] [[Gerry Brownlee]] after refusing to withdraw a comment accusing Seymour of lying about the Treaty of Waitangi. Te Pāti Māori MP [[Hana-Rawhiti Maipi-Clarke]] interrupted parliament's vote on the bill, initiating a protest [[haka]] ("[[Ka Mate]]"), while tearing her copy of the bill in two. She was joined by many Labour, Māori and Green MPs and even the public watching from the gallery. {{As of|2024|11|}}, the video of the haka was viewed more than 700 million times.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |date=19 November 2024 |title=Hana-Rāwhiti Maipi-Clarke and the haka that's been seen 700 million times by a worldwide audience |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/kahu/hana-rawhiti-maipi-clarke-and-the-haka-thats-been-seen-700-million-times-by-a-worldwide-audience/ZPF64PBCKBHBVOB7RISOBZVIPA/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241119082159/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/kahu/hana-rawhiti-maipi-clarke-and-the-haka-thats-been-seen-700-million-times-by-a-worldwide-audience/ZPF64PBCKBHBVOB7RISOBZVIPA/ |archive-date=19 November 2024 |access-date=2024-11-25 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

The haka caused a disruption that delayed parliamentary proceedings for half an hour and led to the temporary suspension of Maipi-Clarke. It also resulted in the referral to the Privileges Committee of Maipi-Clarke, her party co-leaders [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]] and [[Rawiri Waititi]], and Labour MP [[Peeni Henare]], and further suspensions of Maipi-Clarke, Ngarewa-Packer and Waititi (see also: [[Treaty Principles Bill#Privileges Committee hearings|§{{Nbsp}}Privileges Committee hearings]]).<ref name="NZH 1st reading" /><ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last1=Perese |first1=Daniel |title=Watch: Haka erupts in Parliament before Treaty Principles Bill passes first reading |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/11/14/watch-haka-erupts-in-parliament-before-treaty-principles-bill-passes-first-reading/ |access-date=15 November 2024 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |date=14 November 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241115032046/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/11/14/watch-haka-erupts-in-parliament-before-treaty-principles-bill-passes-first-reading/|archive-date=15 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="x957">{{Cite web |title=Conduct of Members—Disruption During Vote |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/combined/HansDeb_20241210_20241210_12 |access-date=5 June 2025 |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]]}}</ref>

When the House resumed, the bill passed its first reading that same evening, 68 votes to 54.

