===Select committee stage===

Public submissions on the Treaty Principles Bill opened on 19{{nbsp}}November 2024 and were expected to close on 7{{nbsp}}January 2025.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Walters |first1=Laura |title=Committee draws short straw to work on Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/11/18/committee-draws-short-straw-to-work-on-treaty-principles-bill/ |access-date=9 December 2024 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=18 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241118013405/https://newsroom.co.nz/2024/11/18/committee-draws-short-straw-to-work-on-treaty-principles-bill/ |archive-date=18 November 2024}}</ref> The [[New Zealand House of Representatives committees#Select committees|select committee]] extended the deadline to 14{{nbsp}}January 2025, to compensate for a website failure.<ref name="green-2025">
{{Cite web
 | last1 = Green | first1 = Kate
 | title = Treaty Principles Bill: number of total submissions to be revealed on Thursday
 | date = 15 January 2025
 | work = RNZ
 | location = Wellington
 | url = https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/538986/treaty-principles-bill-number-of-total-submissions-to-be-revealed-on-thursday
 | access-date = 2025-01-21
}}
</ref>

In mid-December 2024, several local and regional councils including [[Hutt City Council]], [[Auckland Council]], [[Dunedin City Council]], [[Gisborne District Council]], [[Selwyn District Council]], [[Environment Canterbury]] and [[Stratford District Council]] voted to pass motions to send submissions opposing the bill.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Tyson |first1=Jessica |title=Hutt City Council formally opposes Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/12/10/hutt-city-council-first-local-body-to-formally-oppose-treaty-principles-bill/ |access-date=16 December 2024 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |date=10 December 2024 |archive-date=12 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241212140542/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/12/10/hutt-city-council-first-local-body-to-formally-oppose-treaty-principles-bill/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Sam |title=Auckland Council votes to oppose the Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/12/11/auckland-council-votes-to-oppose-the-treaty-principles-bill/ |access-date=10 January 2025 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]]  |date=11 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241215193420/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/12/11/auckland-council-votes-to-oppose-the-treaty-principles-bill/ |archive-date=15 December 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Shaw |first1=Ruby |title=DCC formally opposes Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/dcc/dcc-formally-opposes-treaty-principles-bill |access-date=16 December 2024 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=13 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241213221125/https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/dcc/dcc-formally-opposes-treaty-principles-bill |archive-date=13 December 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Campbell |first1=Zita |title='Standing next to you': Gisborne council's stance against Treaty Bill |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/ldr/536559/standing-next-to-you-gisborne-council-s-stance-against-treaty-bill |access-date=16 December 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241213065727/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/ldr/536559/standing-next-to-you-gisborne-council-s-stance-against-treaty-bill |archive-date=13 December 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Jacobs |first1=Maxine |title=Selwyn council to submit against Treaty Principles Bill after spirited debate |url=https://www.thepress.co.nz/te-ao-maori/360520237/selwyn-council-submit-against-treaty-principles-bill-after-spirited-debate |access-date=16 December 2024 |url-status=live |work=[[The Press]] |date=13 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241215011812/https://www.thepress.co.nz/te-ao-maori/360520237/selwyn-council-submit-against-treaty-principles-bill-after-spirited-debate |archive-date=15 December 2024}}</ref><ref name="Christchurch Star 16 Dec 2024">{{Cite news |title=ECan, Selwyn DC to submit on Treaty Principles Bill but not Ashburton |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/star-news/star-districts/ecan-selwyn-dc-submit-treaty-principles-bill-not-ashburton |access-date=16 December 2024 |work=[[The Star (Christchurch)|The Star]]  |date=16 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241216054524/https://www.odt.co.nz/star-news/star-districts/ecan-selwyn-dc-submit-treaty-principles-bill-not-ashburton |archive-date=16 December 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Hanne |first1=Ilona |title=Stratford council to oppose Treaty Principles Bill over consultation concerns |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/stratford-press/news/stratford-council-to-oppose-treaty-principles-bill-over-consultation-concerns/VKUT3CR5RFHTHPABQUL6IXJ67I/ |access-date=16 December 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=16 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241216054900/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/stratford-press/news/stratford-council-to-oppose-treaty-principles-bill-over-consultation-concerns/VKUT3CR5RFHTHPABQUL6IXJ67I/ |archive-date=16 December 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Ashburton District Council]] declined to make a submission, with Mayor Neil Brown citing "the likelihood of a wide range of views within the community on the bill."<ref name="Christchurch Star 16 Dec 2024" />

Māori lawyer and educator Roimata Smail developed an online submissions template for those opposing the bill. By 9{{nbsp}}January 2025, her webpage had reported 15,905 visits since late November 2024.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Paterson |first1=Te Aniwaniwa |title=Thousands of Kiwis use template to oppose the Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2025/01/09/thousands-of-kiwis-use-template-to-oppose-the-treaty-principles-bill/ |access-date=9 January 2025 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |date=9 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250109074048/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2025/01/09/thousands-of-kiwis-use-template-to-oppose-the-treaty-principles-bill/ |archive-date=9 January 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> A group consisting of 165 descendants of the British missionaries [[Henry Williams (missionary)|Henry]] and [[William Williams (bishop)|William Williams]], who had translated the Treaty of Waitangi, collectively submitted against the bill, saying that it went against "the original intent and integrity of Te Tiriti."<ref>{{Cite news |title=Descendants of Treaty of Waitangi translators speak out against the bill |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/538547/descendants-of-treaty-of-waitangi-translators-speak-out-against-bill |access-date=10 January 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=9 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250110111259/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/538547/descendants-of-treaty-of-waitangi-translators-speak-out-against-bill |archive-date=10 January 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

Public submissions through Parliament's website were due to conclude at 11:59 pm on 7{{nbsp}}January 2025. Hard copies could also be sent by mail or delivered to Parliament until 5pm on 8 January. The large volume of online submissions overwhelmed Parliament's website, with people who were submitting on the night of 6 January and the morning of 7 January encountering error messages or being unable to submit their documents.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Unprecedented number of submissions on Treaty Principles Bill |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/538358/unprecedented-number-of-submissions-on-treaty-principles-bill |access-date=9 January 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=7 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250109073809/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/538358/unprecedented-number-of-submissions-on-treaty-principles-bill |archive-date=9 January 2025}}</ref> In response to the large volume of submissions and technical problems on the website, the justice select committee decided to reopen submissions from 1pm on 9 January and extend the dateline to 1pm on 14 January 2025. By 9{{nbsp}}January, the committee estimated that Parliament had received 300,000 online submissions.<ref name="Stuff 9 Jan 2025">{{Cite news |author1=Stuff reporters |date=9 January 2025 |title=Public submissions on Treaty Principles Bill extended by five days |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360543306/public-submissions-treaty-principles-bill-extended-five-days |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250109074727/https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360543306/public-submissions-treaty-principles-bill-extended-five-days |archive-date=9 January 2025 |access-date=9 January 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]}}</ref>

On 16{{nbsp}}January 2025, the justice select committee convened to consider the bill and confirmed that hearings would commence on 27 January 2025. Without releasing the number of total submissions, the committee estimated that it would listen to 80 hours of oral submissions for a month. To cope with the large number of submissions, the justice committee was split into two sub-committees. The first consisted of Chair [[James Meager]], Deputy Chair [[Tamatha Paul]], [[Todd Stephenson]], [[Ginny Andersen]], [[Paulo Garcia (New Zealand politician)|Paulo Garcia]], [[Tracey McLellan]] while the second consisted of Chair [[Duncan Webb]], Deputy Chair [[Jamie Arbuckle]], [[Tākuta Ferris]], [[Cameron Brewer]] and [[Rima Nakhle]]. Duplicate submissions, those providing first names only, initials only, or pseudonyms, and those containing racist material would be excluded.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Treaty Principles Bill: 80 hours of submissions Justice Committee must hear |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/539162/treaty-principles-bill-80-hours-of-submissions-justice-committee-must-hear |access-date=18 January 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=16 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250118022346/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/539162/treaty-principles-bill-80-hours-of-submissions-justice-committee-must-hear |archive-date=18 January 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> [[The Post (New Zealand newspaper)|''The Post'']] reported that about 70% of the available written submissions were against the bill, with about 20% in support.<ref name="Post 27 Jan 2025">
{{Cite news
 | last1 = Whyte | first1 = Anna
 | title = Political heavyweights turn up heat on Treaty submissions
 | quote = A count by The Post of written submissions available online indicated Monday's submissions were about 70% opposed to the bill and about 20% in support
 | url-status = live
 | url = https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/360559769/political-heavyweights-turn-heat-treaty-submissions-heres-what-theyll-say
 | access-date = 27 January 2025
 | work = [[The Post (New Zealand newspaper)|The Post]]
 | date = 27 January 2025
 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250127045045/https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/360559769/political-heavyweights-turn-heat-treaty-submissions-heres-what-theyll-say
 | archive-date = 27 January 2025
}}
</ref>{{efn|These statistics are likely to be revised upwards in favour of support as better information emerges.}}

Any select committee is responsible for how it handles its submissions, and the Justice Committee chose to reject submissions with "swear words, racist language, [or] abusive comments towards MPs", while allowing form submissions. About 35{{nbsp}}parliamentary officials then attempt to summarise content, record support or opposition, and classify specific themes. The 16,000 requests for oral presentations could not realistically all be serviced, so the committee allowed each political party to nominate 100{{nbsp}}names for consideration.<ref name="collins-2025">
{{Cite web
 | last1 = Collins | first1 = Louis
 | title = The House: the summer of 300,000 submissions
 | date = 9 February 2025
 | work = [[Radio New Zealand]]
 | location = Wellington
 | url = https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/thehouse/541326/the-house-the-summer-of-300-000-submissions
 | access-date = 2025-02-09
}}
