 | url = https://newsroom.co.nz/2025/01/28/experts-political-figures-consider-treaty-principles-debate-beyond-bill/
 | access-date = 2025-02-05
}}
</ref>{{efn|Real{{nbhyph}}time transcript [27 January 2025][1:57:12] "The issues about the referendum, it was extremely concerning in the Waitangi Tribunal to hear that the officials have been asked in parallel to this process to be working on implementation of a referendum approach on this bill. Now we hear this bill is not going to proceed, but that doesn't mean a citizen initiated referendum cannot be instituted and an attempt to have that at the time of the next election. And already the cogs are moving in that direction. That is a major problem."}}

The second day of oral submissions was on Thursday 30{{nbsp}}January 2025. The select committee heard 11{{nbsp}}submitters, with seven opposing the bill and four supporting the bill.<ref name="Newsroom 31 Jan 2025">{{Cite news |last1=Walters |first1=Laura |title=Treaty principles Day 2: Conservation, health, justice highlighted |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2025/01/31/conservation-health-justice-highlighted-in-treaty-principles-hearings/ |access-date=31 January 2025 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=31 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250131021902/https://newsroom.co.nz/2025/01/31/conservation-health-justice-highlighted-in-treaty-principles-hearings/ |archive-date=31 January 2025}}</ref> Former Labour MP and cabinet minister [[Kiri Allan]] denounced the bill as an "abomination", arguing that it sought to erase Māori from New Zealand history, law and their lands.<ref name="Newsroom 31 Jan 2025" /> She also accused the Government of claiming that the Treaty granted "extra benefits" to Māori.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title=Treaty Principles Bill: Former Labour minister Kiri Allan slams 'despicable' Government actions ahead of oral submission |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/treaty-principles-bill-former-labour-minister-kiri-allan-slams-despicable-govt-actions-ahead-of-oral-submission/KPET4VIL4VAL3EQ57ZAH3PUZOY/ |access-date=31 January 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=30 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250131022137/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/treaty-principles-bill-former-labour-minister-kiri-allan-slams-despicable-govt-actions-ahead-of-oral-submission/KPET4VIL4VAL3EQ57ZAH3PUZOY/ |archive-date=31 January 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Retired oil industry economist John Milne supported the bill, explaining that it would mitigate [[political risk|sovereign risk]].<ref name="waiwiri-smith-2025">
{{Cite news
 | last1 = Waiwiri-Smith | first1 = Lyric
 | title = Treaty principles bill hearings, day two: Kiri Allan and Sandra Goudie make submissions
 | date = 30 January 2025
 | work = [[The Spinoff]]
 | location = Auckland, Aotearoa/New Zealand
 | url = https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/30-01-2025/treaty-principles-bill-hearings-day-two-kiri-allan-and-sandra-goudie-make-submissions
 | access-date = 2025-02-01
}}
</ref> Sam Murton and Jo Lambert of the New Zealand Council of Medical Colleges argued that legislating for equality, rather than equity, would lead to poor health outcomes.<ref name="Newsroom 31 Jan 2025" /> YouthLaw general manager Darryn Aitchison said that the bill would have had an adverse impact on Māori youth accessing justice while [[Māori Law Society]] co-president Tai Ahu said that the bill would increase Māori distrust in the government. Former [[District Court of New Zealand|District Court]] judge David Harvey argued that the bill would clarify Treaty principles to the judiciary and also advocated a referendum.<ref name="Newsroom 31 Jan 2025" /> Former MP and former [[Mayor of Thames-Coromandel]] [[Sandra Goudie]] submitted in favour of the bill.<ref name="Newsroom 31 Jan 2025" /> [[University of Auckland]] economist [[Ananish Chaudhuri]] supported the bill, arguing against separate health and educational systems on ethnic lines based on comparisons with India.<ref name="Newsroom 31 Jan 2025" />

Day three of oral submissions took place on 7 February 2025 entirely online with [[Duncan Webb]]{{nbsp}}MP as chair.<ref name="macmanus-2025">
{{Cite news
