=== Polling ===
A Research New Zealand poll conducted in February 2024 showed 36% in support of a referendum, with 35% opposed, the rest undecided.<ref>{{Cite news |date=11 February 2024 |title=Do New Zealanders really want a Treaty referendum? |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/sunday/audio/2018925583/do-new-zealanders-really-want-a-treaty-referendum |access-date=5 April 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz |archive-date=5 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240405102816/https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/sunday/audio/2018925583/do-new-zealanders-really-want-a-treaty-referendum |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=5 April 2024 |title=Act launches campaign to twist National's arm on Treaty referendum |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/act-launches-treaty-principles-referendum-campaign-to-twist-nationals-arm/JUJIBSVQANAVVIOTK3DXPNG62Y/ |access-date=5 April 2024 |work=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ |archive-date=5 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240405102816/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/act-launches-treaty-principles-referendum-campaign-to-twist-nationals-arm/JUJIBSVQANAVVIOTK3DXPNG62Y/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

An October 2024 poll by Curia and commissioned by the [[New Zealand Taxpayers' Union]] showed 46% supported the Treaty Principles Bill, 25% opposed it, and 29% were unsure.<ref name="poll2"/> The poll also found that New Zealand First voters were most supportive and Te Pāti Māori voters were the least supportive of the bill.<ref name="poll2">{{Cite web | url=https://thefacts.nz/21-voters-still-support-the-reworded-treaty-principles-bill-41-national-51-nz-first-11-labour-greens | title=~2:1 voters support the reworded Treaty Principles Bill (~4:1 Nat, 5:1 NZF, 2:1 A, 1:1 L/G) | date=14 October 2024 | publisher=New Zealand Taxpayer's Union }}</ref>

A 1News-Verian poll conducted between 30 November and 4 December 2024 showed 23% of participants supported the bill, while 36% opposed it, and 39% said they did not know enough about it.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2024-12-10 |title=1News-Verian poll suggests more Kiwis oppose Treaty Principles Bill than support it |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/536273/1news-verian-poll-suggests-more-kiwis-oppose-treaty-principles-bill-than-support-it |access-date=2024-12-13 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz |archive-date=13 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241213083854/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/536273/1news-verian-poll-suggests-more-kiwis-oppose-treaty-principles-bill-than-support-it |url-status=live }}</ref>

A second public opinion poll by Curia and commissioned by the ACT party between 1 December and 3 December 2024 showed 39% supporting the bill, while 36% opposed it, and 25% were unsure.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Farrar |first1=David |title=Treaty Issues Poll December 2024 |url=https://assets.nationbuilder.com/actnz/mailings/14478/attachments/original/Treaty_Poll_ACT_Dec24.pdf?1734061223 |website=Nationbuilder.com |publisher=Curia Market Research |access-date=18 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250111224349/https://assets.nationbuilder.com/actnz/mailings/14478/attachments/original/Treaty_Poll_ACT_Dec24.pdf?1734061223 |archive-date=11 January 2025 |date=4 December 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Protests===
{{Main|Hīkoi mō te Tiriti}}
[[File:"Honour it, don't edit it" sign at Toitū Te Tiriti protest in Heretaunga (Hastings) 16 November 2024.jpg|thumb|Hīkoi protest in [[Hastings, New Zealand|Hastings]], November 2024]]
[[File:Māori woman holding honor the Treaty placard Waitangi Day 2024 Wellington.jpg|thumb|A Māori woman holds a placard reading "Toitu te Tiriti" ("Honour the Treaty") in Wellington on 6{{nbsp}}February 2024]]

On 7 November, protesters gathered outside the [[New Zealand Parliament]] and Seymour's electorate office in [[Epsom, New Zealand|Epsom]] to protest against its introduction.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title=Treaty Principles Bill legislation introduced with narrowed wording, protesters gather outside Parliament |url-status=live |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/treaty-principles-bill-protesters-at-parliament-ahead-of-todays-legislation-unveiling/3EXVIASIO5AXLEIHKYEFWOGCLA/ |access-date=9 November 2024 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=7 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241109104005/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/treaty-principles-bill-protesters-at-parliament-ahead-of-todays-legislation-unveiling/3EXVIASIO5AXLEIHKYEFWOGCLA/ |archive-date=9 November 2024}}</ref>

From 11 November, a [[hīkoi]] (march) opposing the legislation called ''[[Hīkoi mō te Tiriti]]'' (March for the Treaty) began marching to Parliament in Wellington in two convoys beginning at [[Cape Reinga]] in [[Northland Region|Northland]] and [[Bluff, New Zealand|Bluff]] in [[Southland Region|Southland]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Live updates: Treaty Principles Bill hīkoi day one |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/11/11/live-updates-treaty-principles-bill-hikoi-day-one/ |access-date=13 November 2024 |work=Te Ao Māori News |publisher=[[Whakaata Māori]] |date=11 November 2024 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241113110445/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/11/11/live-updates-treaty-principles-bill-hikoi-day-one/ |archive-date=13 November 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Tapu |first1=Nina |title=Hikoi starts in far South |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/southland/hikoi-starts-far-south |access-date=13 November 2024 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=12 November 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241113110647/https://www.odt.co.nz/southland/hikoi-starts-far-south|archive-date=13 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>
North Island Hīkoi supporters reached [[Whangārei]] on 11 November before travelling to [[Dargaville]] and Auckland's [[North Shore, New Zealand|North Shore]] on 12 November. Organisers also worked with Police to minimise traffic disruption.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Hīkoi mō te Tiriti day one: 'Lets make this hīkoi build a nation' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/533479/hikoi-mo-te-tiriti-day-one-lets-make-this-hikoi-build-a-nation |access-date=13 November 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=12 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241113115846/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-manu-korihi/533479/hikoi-mo-te-tiriti-day-one-lets-make-this-hikoi-build-a-nation|archive-date=13 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> By 13 November, the North Island convoy had crossed the [[Auckland Harbour Bridge]] and reached [[Ihumātao]] and [[Bastion Point]].<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Arambepola |first1=Sandra |title=In pictures: Hīkoi mō te Tiriti crosses Auckland on its way to Wellington |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360485346/pictures-hikoi-mo-te-tiriti-crosses-auckland-its-way-wellington |access-date=13 November 2024 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=13 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241113111031/https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360485346/pictures-hikoi-mo-te-tiriti-crosses-auckland-its-way-wellington|archive-date=13 November 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url-status=live |title=Hīkoi mō te Tiriti departs Parliament after petition presented |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/11/11/live-national-toitu-te-tiriti-hikoi-travels-the-country/ |access-date=13 November 2024 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date=13 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241113120159/https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/11/11/live-national-toitu-te-tiriti-hikoi-travels-the-country/ |archive-date=13 November 2024}}</ref>

The haka and hīkoi in opposition to the bill also gained the support of singer [[Lorde]], [[Chris Martin]] of [[Coldplay]], and actors [[Jason Momoa]] and [[Octavia Spencer]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-11-15 |title=Coldplay's Chris Martin, Jason Momoa and other stars share support for hīkoi, haka and Māori |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/entertainment/hikoi-mo-te-tiriti-chris-martin-of-coldplay-jason-momoa-octavia-spencer-show-support-for-haka-and-maori/S5VAX2DL5JAABK5DWTUGIZHFGM/ |access-date=2024-11-25 |website=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ}}</ref> [[New Zealand national rugby union team|All Blacks]] player [[TJ Perenara]] incorporated a reference to the hīkoi when he led the haka for the national rugby team. He said it was important for him to acknowledge the hīkoi and the national unity at this time.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-11-22 |title='Important to me': TJ Perenara's final haka tribute to Treaty protest |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/rugby/all-blacks/tj-perenaras-haka-tribute-to-treaty-protest-highlights-all-blacks-win-over-italy/ZJE6B2A2EJCDXG6BADZPHIEXQQ/ |access-date=2024-11-25 |website=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ}}</ref> There was more non-Māori support for the hīkoi than previous protests such as the Foreshore and Seabed hīkoi of 2004.<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 November 2024 |title=The biggest difference between Hīkoi mō te Tiriti and past hīkoi: More support from non-Māori |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/534466/the-biggest-difference-between-hikoi-mo-te-tiriti-and-past-hikoi-more-support-from-non-maori |access-date=25 November 2024 |website=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz |archive-date=26 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241126225618/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/534466/the-biggest-difference-between-hikoi-mo-te-tiriti-and-past-hikoi-more-support-from-non-maori |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 16 November, [[Brian Tamaki]] led a convoy down an Auckland motorway as a counter protest to the nationwide hīkoi, supporting the Treaty Principles Bill.<ref>{{Cite news |title='Unlawful and extremely irresponsible': Police slam Brian Tamaki's motorway convoy |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/360489266/patriots-parade-travels-down-auckland-motorway |access-date=16 November 2024 |work=Stuff |first=Brianna |last=McIlraith}}</ref>

On 19 November 2024, tens of thousands of New Zealanders, including the Māori queen [[Nga wai hono i te po]], joined in nationwide protest, converging in Wellington to protest the bill, marking one of the largest demonstrations in the country's history.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Lagan |first=Bernard |date=2024-11-19 |title=New Zealand protest: march against bill to water down Maori rights |url= https://www.thetimes.com/world/australasia/article/new-zealanders-march-against-bill-to-water-down-treaty-3bxxwt6sj |access-date=2024-11-25 |website=www.thetimes.com |archive-date=25 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241125141656/https://www.thetimes.com/world/australasia/article/new-zealanders-march-against-bill-to-water-down-treaty-3bxxwt6sj |url-status=live }}</ref> A international petition led by youth from the iwi [[Ngāti Whakaue]] was also presented which by 25 November 2024, had amassed 289,000 signatures.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stop the Treaty Principles Bill – Toitū Te Tiriti!! |url=https://our.actionstation.org.nz/petitions/kati-stop-the-introduction-of-the-treaty-principles-bill |access-date=2024-11-25 |website=OurActionStation |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2025-04-11 |title=Treaty Principles Bill scrapped but the fight for Te Tiriti is just beginning - lawyers and advocates say |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/557820/treaty-principles-bill-scrapped-but-the-fight-for-te-tiriti-is-just-beginning-lawyers-and-advocates-say |access-date=2025-05-03 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref> It was the second largest petition in New Zealand's history.<ref name=":1" />

== Privileges Committee hearings ==
Four referrals were made by the [[Speaker of the New Zealand House of Representatives|Speaker]], [[Gerry Brownlee]], to parliament's Privileges Committee for alleged breaches of privilege and disorderly conduct made during the bill's first reading debate. The specific actions under question were the interruption of the vote by Te Pāti Māori MP [[Hana-Rawhiti Maipi-Clarke]] with the "[[Ka Mate]]" [[haka]] and the movement onto the House floor by Maipi-Clarke, [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]] and [[Rawiri Waititi]], and Labour MP [[Peeni Henare]].<ref name="NZH 1st reading" /><ref name=":3" /> Te Pāti Māori MPs moved toward ACT New Zealand MPs and Ngarewa-Packer made a gesture that was later described as a "[[finger gun]]" firing.<ref>{{Cite news |date=20 May 2025 |title=Te Pāti Māori voices in Parliament on Budget day in doubt after compromise talks fail |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/tin-pot-dictatorship-te-pati-maori-voices-in-parliament-on-budget-day-in-doubt-after-compromise-talks-fail/UB65LHQKQJAPVPP22UKRR6VPEU/ |url-access=subscription |access-date=20 May 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |quote=Ngarewa-Packer pointed at Act MPs using a gesture "similar to a finger gun", the committee’s report said, repeating the gesture at the end of the haka and "simulating a firing motion".}}</ref>

Over a series of meetings and in two reports, the Privileges Committee chaired by [[Attorney-General (New Zealand)|Attorney-General]] [[Judith Collins]] recommended that Henare be required to apologise to the House for interrupting a vote, that Maipi-Clarke be suspended from the House for seven days for acting in a manner that could intimidate another member, and that Ngarewa-Packer and Waititi be suspended for 21 days for acting in a manner that could intimidate another member.<ref name="b432">{{Cite web |last=Privileges Committee |date=March 2025 |title=Question of privilege concerning the conduct of four members during proceedings of the House (interim report) |url=https://selectcommittees.parliament.nz/view/SelectCommitteeReport/2ee20cd0-654e-4476-ddf2-08dd6bdc83f8 |access-date=5 June 2025}}</ref><ref name="d949">{{Cite web |last=Privileges Committee |date=May 2025 |title=Question of privilege concerning the conduct of four members during proceedings of the House (final report) |url=https://selectcommittees.parliament.nz/view/SelectCommitteeReport/24d21814-f7c4-4bc4-c83a-08dd9330fa9f |access-date=5 June 2025}}</ref> Henare apologised to the committee during its deliberations and apologised to the House on 25 March 2025.<ref name="t020">{{Cite web |title=Privileges Committee Report—Member Conduct |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/combined/HansDeb_20250325_20250326_38 |access-date=5 June 2025 |website=New Zealand Parliament}}</ref>

The three Te Pāti Māori MPs were invited to appear before the committee. They sought to appear together, not individually, and be represented by legal counsel and tikanga experts; all of which were denied. They later agreed to provide written evidence. The MPs' letters to the committee focused on the conflict between expressions of tikanga and the rules of the House. They refused to apologise for their actions.<ref name="d949" />

Following more than three hours of debate, on two sitting days, Parliament voted along party lines on 5 June 2025 to suspend Maipi-Clarke for seven days and Ngarewa-Packer and Waititi for 21 days.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Ng |first1=Kelly |date=5 June 2025 |title=Three Maori MPs suspended over 'intimidating' haka |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yg9k8x8pwo |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250605124521/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yg9k8x8pwo |archive-date=5 June 2025 |access-date=5 June 2025 |work=[[BBC News]]}}</ref><ref name="n187">{{Cite web |title=Consideration of Report of Privileges Committee |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/combined/HansDeb_20250605_20250605_20 |access-date=5 June 2025 |website=New Zealand Parliament}}</ref>

